

**Example 1.** Lemmiscatic Case

(a) Given _z_=_x_+_iy_ in the Fundamental Triangle, find \(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{D}^{\prime},\xi,\sigma)\) more accurately than can be done with the maps.

\(\sigma\)--Use Maclaurin series throughout the Fundamental Triangle. Five terms give at least six significant figures, six terms at least ten. \(\mathcal{D}\), \(\xi\)--Use Laurent's series directly "near" 0, (if \(|z|\)\(<\)\(1\), four terms give at least eight significant figures for \(\mathcal{D}\), nine for \(\xi\); five terms at least ten significant figures for \(\mathcal{D}\), eleven for \(\xi\)). Use Taylor's series directly "near" \(z_{6}\). Elsewhere (unless approximately seven or eight significant figures are insufficient) use economized polynomials to obtain \(\mathcal{D}(x)\), \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}(x)\) and/or \(\xi(x)\) as appropriate. To get \(\mathcal{D}(iy)\), \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}(iy)\) and/or \(\xi(iy)\), use Laurent's series for "small" \(y\), otherwise use economized polynomials to compute \(\mathcal{D}(y)\), \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}(y)\) and/or \(\xi(y)\), then use complex multiplication to obtain \(\mathcal{D}(iy)\), \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}(iy)\) and/or \(\xi(iy)\). Finally, use appropriate addition formula to get \(\mathcal{D}(z)\) and/or \(\xi(z)\).

\(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}\)--Use Laurent's series directly "near" 0 (if \(|z|\)\(<\)\(1\), four terms give at least six significant figures, five terms at least eight significant figures). Elsewhere, either use economized polynomials and addition formula as for \(\mathcal{D}\) and \(\xi\), or get \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime\prime}\)\(=\)\(4\mathcal{D}^{\prime}\)\(=\)\(\mathcal{D}\) and extract appropriate square root (\(\mathcal{D}\mathcal{D}^{\prime}\)\(\geq\)\(0\)).

(b) Given \(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{D}^{\prime},\xi,\sigma)\) corresponding to a point in the Fundamental Triangle, compute \(z\) more accurately than can be done with the maps. Only a few significant figures are obtainable from the use of any of the given (truncated) reversed series, except in a small neighborhood of the center of the series.

**For greater accuracy, use inverse interpolation procedures.**

**Example 2.** Equianharmonic Case

(a) Given _z_=_x_+_iy_ in the Fundamental Triangle, find \(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{D}^{\prime},\xi,\sigma)\) more accurately than can be done with the maps.

\(\sigma\)--Use Maclaurin series throughout the Fundamental Triangle. Four terms give at least eleven significant figures, five terms at least twenty one.

\(\mathcal{D}\),\(\xi\)--Use Laurent's series directly "near" 0 (if \(|z|\)\(<\)\(1\), four terms give at least \(10\)S for \(\mathcal{D}\), \(11\)S for \(\xi\); five terms at least \(13\)S for \(\mathcal{D}\), \(14\)S for \(\xi\)). Elsewhere (unless approximately seven or eight significant figures are insufficient) use economized polynomials to obtain \(\mathcal{D}(x)\), \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}(x)\) and/or \(\xi(x)\), as appropriate. To get \(\mathcal{D}(iy)\), \(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}(iy)\) and/or \(\xi(iy)\), use Laurent's series. Then use appropriate addition formula to get \(\mathcal{D}(z)\) and/or \(\xi(z)\).

